## Why This Is Asked
This question introduces a data-analytics angle (ADLS Gen2 + Data Explorer) and governance concerns not covered by prior prompts. It tests knowledge of data flows, security, and cost controls without overloading a single service.

## Key Concepts
- Azure Data Lake Storage Gen2 ingestion patterns
- Azure Data Explorer (Kusto) for dashboards
- Event-driven integration (Event Grid)
- TLS termination and Front Door
- GitHub Actions CI/CD
- Resource tagging and budget alerts
- Lightweight disaster recovery

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example: Function writes raw feedback to ADLS Gen2 using @azure/storage-blob
const { BlobServiceClient } = require("@azure/storage-blob");
module.exports = async function(context, req) {
  const blobClient = BlobServiceClient.fromConnectionString(process.env.AZURE_STORAGE_CONNECTION_STRING);
  const container = blobClient.getContainerClient("raw-feedback");
  const blockBlob = container.getBlockBlobClient(`${Date.now()}-feedback.json`);
  await blockBlob.upload(JSON.stringify(req.body), Buffer.byteLength(JSON.stringify(req.body)));
  return { status: 200, body: "ok" };
};
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle schema evolution for feedback records?
- What are the latency and pricing trade-offs between ADLS Gen2 vs Data Explorer ingestion?
- How would you implement data retention and access controls?
